Excerpts from netnews.rec.games.hack: 10-May-93 NetHack observations,
take .. by Fred Sloniker@u.washingt 
> * I tested in wizard mode, forcing the gods to be pleased, and they *still*
>   wouldn't cure my blindness.  This seems like a bug to me; what's the point
>   of divine intervention if they don't think blindness is a problem?

The gods will cure blindness.  It's on the list of problems to be fixed
(there is a heirarchy of stuff they'll cure in order...it starts with
strangulation I believe, goes to turning to stone and goes down from
there).  It is one of the last things they'll fix, however.  I'm not
sure why they wouldn't cure you, but in my current game I remember
distinctly getting my blindness cured at one point (and getting healed,
getting stats restored and being granted stealth...my god likes me).

> * I bought out the candle shop, and trekked to my altar to identify the
>   candles.  I had four uncursed candles, two uncursed candles, and two
>   blessed candles.  Can candles have plusses or something?  (They wouldn't
>   combine into one slot.)  And do I need to get at least seven in one slot
>   to load the Candelabra?

If you really want to blow an identify scroll, identify the candles. 
You'll discover that some are tallow and some are wax.  It doesn't
matter for the Candelabra, but it chews up a couple inventory slots.

> * I still want to be able to attack doors and statues!  Make some use out
>   of Vorpal Blade besides turning monsters into so much vat gruel.

Well, you can SORT of attack statues by applying a pick axe to them, but
I agree with you.  This should, however, dull your blade (item idea: 
whetstone.  It would be a gray stone, kickable, and would restore any
plusses taken away by dulling a blade.)

> * I am *really sick* of discovering a falling-rock trap and knowing my pet
>   is doomed.  If I've discovered a trap, my pet should either automatically
>   avoid it, or at least I should be able to warn it or something.  (The
>   first is probably a lot easier to implement.)

Ah, who cares about pets.  :)

Seriously, I don't know if dogs and cats should be intelligent enough to
avoid traps ("Rover!  Bad dog!  No step on trap!"), but other pets (like
your pet gray dragon, Ed) should be able to figure it out.  Of course,
you would then have to divide pets into "intelligent", "not so
intelligent" and "dumb as a post" which would probably be a pain in the
ass.  There is a way to stop your pet from trodding on the falling rock
trap.  Just step onto the trap (<poink>) and drop a cursed item.  I've
never understood HOW a pet knows if something is cursed, but there you
go.

> * What the heck are shriekers, anyway?  They don't do damage, they don't
>   kill attributes... do they curse or something?

I think the only thing they do is wake up any sleeping monsters on the
level and probably cause them to start moving towards you ala bugles and
whistles.  They also happen to be good eatin' (risk free poison
resistance) so I'm not complaining.

